(function () {
  window.AIPO = window.AIPO || {};
  window.AIPO.lessons = window.AIPO.lessons || [];
  window.AIPO.scenarios = window.AIPO.scenarios || {};
  window.AIPO.why = window.AIPO.why || {};
  window.AIPO.questions = window.AIPO.questions || [];
  window.AIPO.cards = window.AIPO.cards || [];
  function S(id, d, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "single", stem: stem, options: options, correct: [correct], explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  function M(id, d, pick, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "multi", pick: pick, stem: stem, options: options, correct: correct, explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }

  window.AIPO.lessons.push(
    {
      "id": "frame-1",
      "n": 1,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Start from the user problem, not the technology",
      "body": "Many AI efforts begin with a sentence such as 'we need an AI feature'. That starts from a solution. The Google People + AI Guidebook, in its chapter User Needs + Defining Success (paraphrased here as concept guidance), suggests another opening: find a real problem that people need help with, then ask how it might be solved and whether AI could solve it in a unique way. Compare 'Can we use AI to...?' with 'How might we solve...?'. The second keeps every option open, including non-AI ones.\n\nTalking to people, observing behaviour and looking through data are the usual ways to find it. Mapping the current workflow step by step shows where effort, delay or error really sit and which steps could be automated or helped. PAIR names a 'Wizard of Oz' test as a way to see how people feel about automating part of a process; in common usage a person performs the step behind the scenes, which is general practice and lets a team learn before building. Hear from a diverse set of users early, so that some groups are not left out by accident.\n\nNIST AI RMF 1.0 supports the same habit. MAP 1.1 asks that intended purposes, the types of users and their expectations, and potential positive and negative impacts are understood and documented. MAP 1.4 asks that the business value is clearly defined, or re-evaluated when an existing system is assessed. As practical judgement, when a problem statement rests on opinion alone, the PO should pause and gather evidence before committing to a solution.",
      "points": [
        "Ask 'How might we solve this problem?' before 'Can we use AI?'.",
        "Find the problem through conversations, observation and data.",
        "Map the current workflow to see where help is really needed.",
        "Test reactions to automation cheaply, for example with a simulated step.",
        "Document purpose, users, expectations and business value (MAP 1.1, 1.4)."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Starting from a sponsor's wish for 'an AI feature'.",
        "Treating a stakeholder's opinion as a confirmed user problem.",
        "Interviewing only the easiest or most vocal users.",
        "Never revisiting the business value of a feature already live."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Google People + AI Guidebook, chapter User Needs + Defining Success (concepts paraphrased guidance; the local PDF may be an older edition than the live guidebook)",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.1",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.4",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023; PAIR guidebook (edition may differ from live)",
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "frame-2",
      "n": 2,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Does AI add unique value, or would a rule do?",
      "body": "Once a real problem is clear, the next question is whether AI is the right tool. A rule or heuristic is a fixed piece of logic written by people, such as 'if the field is empty, show an error'. The PAIR chapter User Needs + Defining Success (paraphrased) points out that a rule often works as well as an AI version, or better, and is easier to build, explain, debug and maintain. A simpler non-AI approach is therefore a legitimate and frequently correct answer.\n\nThe chapter lists situations where AI is probably the better fit: personalised recommendations, predicting future events, understanding natural language, recognising a whole class of things, and detecting rare events that keep changing, such as fraud. It also lists situations where AI is probably not better: when predictability is the value, when information is static or limited, when costly errors outweigh a small gain, when complete transparency is needed, when speed and low cost matter above all, and when people have said they do not want the task changed.\n\nNIST AI RMF 1.0 points the same way. MAP 2.1 asks that the specific tasks, and the methods used to implement them, are defined, for example classifiers, generative models or recommenders. MANAGE 2.1 asks that managing risk takes account of viable non-AI alternatives that could reduce the size or likelihood of impacts. As good practice, test a simple baseline first, and let AI earn its place by showing it adds value beyond it.",
      "points": [
        "A rule or heuristic is often simpler to build, explain, debug and maintain.",
        "AI tends to fit personalisation, prediction, language, class recognition and changing rare events.",
        "AI is probably not better where predictability, transparency or very costly errors dominate.",
        "Define the specific tasks and methods the system will support (MAP 2.1).",
        "Consider viable non-AI alternatives when managing risk (MANAGE 2.1)."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Choosing AI because it is modern or because competitors have it.",
        "Comparing AI options only against each other, never against a simple rule.",
        "Accepting a small average gain while ignoring severe rare errors.",
        "Treating 'no AI' as a failure of ambition."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Google People + AI Guidebook, chapter User Needs + Defining Success (concepts paraphrased guidance; the local PDF may be an older edition than the live guidebook)",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 2.1",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MANAGE 2.1",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023; PAIR guidebook (edition may differ from live)",
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "frame-3",
      "n": 3,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Automate the task or augment the person?",
      "body": "When AI is the right approach, there is still a design choice. Automation means the system does the task for the person. Augmentation means the system helps the person do the task themselves, faster or better. The PAIR chapter User Needs + Defining Success (paraphrased) suggests automation when a task is boring, repetitive, awkward or dangerous, when people lack the knowledge or ability to do it, or when they simply want it gone. It is helped further when the people who do the task today agree on the correct way to do it.\n\nIt suggests augmentation when people enjoy the task, when personal responsibility for the outcome matters, when the stakes are high for the person, or when their preferences are hard to put into words. If people say plainly that they do not want a task automated or helped by AI, treat that as a signal worth exploring before committing.\n\nEven when a task is automated, the chapter says there should almost always be a way for people to oversee it and step in, for example by previewing, editing or undoing. The research questions it suggests, such as which duties a person would give to a human assistant, help to learn what people want to hand over. Where a personal responsibility may be required by law, that is a question for legal and compliance specialists, not one the PO settles alone.",
      "points": [
        "Automate tasks that are tedious, dangerous, beyond people's skill or agreed in method.",
        "Augment tasks people enjoy, own personally, find high stakes or struggle to describe.",
        "Ask users which duties they would give a human assistant.",
        "Keep a route for oversight and intervention even in automated tasks.",
        "Involve legal and compliance specialists when responsibility may be a legal matter."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Assuming automation is always the best help for users.",
        "Ignoring people who say they do not want the task changed.",
        "Automating with no way to preview, edit or undo.",
        "Settling a possible legal responsibility question without specialists."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Google People + AI Guidebook, chapter User Needs + Defining Success (concepts paraphrased guidance; the local PDF may be an older edition than the live guidebook)",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023; PAIR guidebook (edition may differ from live)",
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "frame-4",
      "n": 4,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Define success with the team: the reward function and the cost of errors",
      "body": "Every AI model is guided by something that tells it what counts as right or wrong. In PAIR terms (chapter User Needs + Defining Success, paraphrased) this is the reward function, also called the objective or loss function. It shapes what the model tries to optimise and so much of the user experience. PAIR says it should be designed together by a cross-functional team, with UX, product and engineering views at a minimum, and imagines downstream effects over the long term.\n\nA key step is weighing errors. A false positive is a wrong 'yes', such as a false alarm. A false negative is a wrong 'no', such as a missed case. Treating both as equal by default rarely matches real consequences, so the team should discuss which hurts more. Precision and recall trade against each other, and PAIR notes there is no way around that trade-off, so test the balance with users.\n\nA simple, narrow, immediate measure can cause harm over time. A useful check is to ask what would happen to users and to society if the measure were perfectly optimised. PAIR suggests agreeing a statement of the form: if a specific metric crosses a meaningful threshold, we will take a specific action. It also suggests checking impact on a regular schedule, and speaking with any group that is harmed. The PO helps the team agree this definition. Model evaluation itself is covered in domain 5.",
      "points": [
        "The reward function defines what the model optimises and shapes the user experience.",
        "Design it with UX, product and engineering together, thinking long term.",
        "Weigh false positives and false negatives by their real consequences.",
        "Precision and recall trade off, so test the balance with users.",
        "Agree a metric, a threshold and an action, and check impact on a schedule."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Using one simple, immediate measure such as time spent or shares.",
        "Weighing both kinds of error equally by default.",
        "Letting data scientists define success alone.",
        "Setting no action to take when the measure goes wrong."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Google People + AI Guidebook, chapter User Needs + Defining Success (concepts paraphrased guidance; the local PDF may be an older edition than the live guidebook)",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023; PAIR guidebook (edition may differ from live)",
      "reviewed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "frame-5",
      "n": 5,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Write it down: purpose, benefits, costs of errors and scope",
      "body": "Framing is not finished until it is written down where others can see it. NIST AI RMF 1.0 is useful here. MAP 1.1 asks that intended purposes, beneficial uses, types of users and their expectations, potential positive and negative impacts, and assumptions and limitations are understood and documented. MAP 3.1 asks that the potential benefits of the intended functionality and performance are examined and documented. MAP 3.2 asks the same for potential costs of expected or realised errors, including non-monetary costs, connected to organisational risk tolerance.\n\nNon-monetary costs are those without a price tag, such as lost trust, distress, unfair treatment or harm to a person. A case listing only benefits cannot show that the trade-off is worthwhile. MAP 1.5 asks that organisational risk tolerances are determined and documented, and GOVERN 2.3 says executive leadership takes responsibility for decisions about AI risks. As practical judgement, the PO therefore does not set risk tolerance alone, and brings evidence and escalates.\n\nFinally, MAP 3.3 asks that the targeted application scope is specified and documented, based on the system's capability, established context and categorisation. As practical judgement, a clear scope also says what the system will not do. As practical judgement, a new language, group or task is a new condition: treat it as out of scope until it has been evaluated, and involve the relevant specialists when it is high stakes or touches personal data.",
      "points": [
        "Document purpose, users, expectations, impacts and assumptions (MAP 1.1).",
        "Examine and document benefits (MAP 3.1) and costs of errors (MAP 3.2).",
        "Include non-monetary costs such as trust, distress and harm.",
        "Settle risk tolerance with the people accountable for risk.",
        "State scope from capability and context, including what is out of scope (MAP 3.3)."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "A business case with benefits and no costs.",
        "Counting only monetary costs of errors.",
        "Setting risk tolerance alone as the PO.",
        "Letting scope grow to new users or tasks without evaluation."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.1",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 1.5",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 3.1",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 3.2",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), MAP 3.3",
        "NIST AI RMF 1.0 (AI 100-1, Jan 2023), GOVERN 2.3",
        "General practice, not from a source"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "AI RMF 1.0, Jan 2023",
      "reviewed": false
    }
  );

  window.AIPO.scenarios.scFR1 = {"title":"Reducing missed clinic appointments","text":"A clinic network loses many appointment slots to patients who do not attend. The scheduling lead proposes an AI system that predicts likely no-shows and automatically double-books those slots. So far the team has no notes from conversations with patients or front-desk staff, and the only data is last year's booking export, which includes some health-related details. Front-desk staff say most misses happen because patients forget, and reminders currently go out only by letter. The privacy team has not yet been consulted."};
  window.AIPO.scenarios.scFR2 = {"title":"An internal help assistant for expense questions","text":"An organisation plans an internal help assistant for expense questions. Support tickets show that about half of all questions repeat the same dozen policy questions, each with a fixed answer. The rest are unusual cases that need a judgement by the finance team. The sponsor's stated goal is fewer tickets. The policy wording changes a few times a year. A wrong answer could lead an employee to submit a claim that is rejected or that breaks a rule."};

  window.AIPO.questions.push(
    S("fr001", "frame", "A sponsor tells a Product Owner, 'We need an AI feature in the app this quarter.' Support data shows users abandon the booking step, but nobody has asked them why. Which first step is best?", ["Ask engineering which AI technique could give the sponsor the quickest demo this quarter","Draft and size a backlog of model features so the team can check its capacity for the quarter","Ask which user problem it should solve and check how people cope with it today","Pick a supplier after a short review so the work can be agreed before the quarter starts"], 2, "Starting from the user problem keeps every solution open, including ones without AI. A technique, a backlog or a supplier all assume the answer before the problem is understood."),
    S("fr002", "frame", "A team is kicking off discovery for a new feature and wants to open with the question PAIR suggests for exploring human-centred AI solutions. Which question should they use?", ["How might we solve this user problem, and could AI solve it in a unique way?","Can we use AI to speed this process up, and which approach should we test first?","Which AI approach is most popular for ideas like ours, and who has used it?","What would it cost to copy a rival's AI feature, and how soon could we ship it?"], 0, "PAIR contrasts 'Can we use AI to...?' with 'How might we solve...?' and then asks whether AI can solve it in a unique way. The other questions begin with the technology, its popularity or a rival's product."),
    S("fr003", "frame", "A stakeholder insists users hate the checkout page, but no one has watched users or reviewed data. A redesign slot opens in two sprints. What should the PO do?", ["Commission an AI model to personalise the page, then compare it with the current page","Redesign the page to match the stakeholder's view and review the result after release","Add the issue to the backlog as a confirmed problem and ask the team to estimate it and agree the date","Spend a sprint observing users and checking behaviour data, then choose the fix"], 3, "A problem statement needs evidence. Observing people and looking at data can shift thinking from technology-first to people-first, and may show the real cause is something else."),
    S("fr004", "frame", "A PO has interviewed three staff about a manual intake task and now plans to map the task step by step. What is the main benefit of the map?", ["It confirms that AI is the right answer and shows which model to test first","It shows where effort, delay or error really sits and which steps could be helped","It gives the model team a ready-made training set from the recorded steps","It replaces further conversations, because the map records what people do"], 1, "A workflow map reveals steps and pain points so the team can see where help is needed. It does not decide the solution, supply data on its own or replace talking to users."),
    M("fr005", "frame", 2, "Which TWO activities best move a team from technology-first to people-first thinking?", ["Observe people doing the task in their own setting","Benchmark which AI features competitors have released and review their results","Ask engineers which techniques they would like to try and test first","Review data on how the task is done today","Write the model requirements and define the metrics before meeting users"], [0,3], "Talking to people, observing behaviour and looking through data ground the problem in real needs. Competitor features, engineer preferences and early model requirements start from the solution side."),
    S("fr006", "frame", "A team wants to learn whether people would accept an automated step before building it. About 20 staff use the step daily. Which approach fits?", ["Build a working model first, then ask staff for their feedback","Survey stakeholders on whether staff would accept the step, and document the answers","Have a person carry out the step behind the scenes and watch how staff react","Release a basic version to the whole team and see what complaints follow"], 2, "PAIR names the Wizard of Oz test as a way to see how people feel about automating part of a process. The usual meaning, a person quietly performing the step, is general practice rather than PAIR wording, and it lets a team learn before committing to build."),
    S("fr007", "frame", "A team proposes an AI model to check that a postcode field has the right format. The format is fixed and published, and the form goes live next sprint. What is the best response?", ["Decline the model and ship a simple format rule with the form","Approve the model and handle edge cases as they appear over time","Approve the model so the team builds experience, and review the cost afterwards","Run a model comparison first and check which one scores best on the format"], 0, "When information is static and limited, a rule is easier to build, explain, debug and maintain. PAIR lists this as a case where AI is probably not better."),
    S("fr008", "frame", "A team is deciding where to apply AI first. Which situation is the strongest case for AI instead of fixed rules?", ["Checking opening hours that stay the same at each branch","Keeping a Cancel button in a predictable place on each screen","Applying a documented fee table to an order","Flagging fraud that is rare per person and keeps changing, where fixed rules need constant review"], 3, "PAIR lists detection of low-occurrence events that change over time as a good fit. Static information, predictable controls and fixed tables suit rules."),
    S("fr009", "frame", "An AI route suggestion would save drivers a few seconds per trip but sometimes sends them down unsafe tracks, about 1 trip in 200 in testing. What does the framing guidance point to?", ["Launch the AI version because its average accuracy beats the simpler routing, and monitor incidents weekly","Keep the simpler routing and decline the AI version, since costly errors outweigh a small gain","Launch the AI version and assess each unsafe case as it appears in the field","Ask the model team to raise the success rate slightly, then retry"], 1, "PAIR lists minimising costly errors as a situation where AI is probably not better: a small increase in success rate does not justify a very high cost of errors."),
    M("fr010", "frame", 2, "Which TWO situations suggest AI is probably NOT the better choice?", ["Different users need personalised suggestions based on evidence of past use","Users or developers must understand precisely what the system does, and check it","The system must understand natural language, and teams will test many speech styles","A whole class of items must be recognised and checked, not just known examples","The most valuable part of the experience is that it behaves predictably, as agreed"], [1,4], "PAIR lists complete transparency and predictability among situations where a rule is probably better. Personalisation, language understanding and class recognition are cases where AI often adds value."),
    S("fr011", "frame", "A team is writing the risk response plan for a proposed AI feature. Under MANAGE 2.1 in the NIST AI RMF, what should be weighed alongside the resources needed to manage AI risks?", ["Whether a competitor has launched a similar feature in the market","How much effort the team has already spent in earlier sprints","Viable non-AI alternatives that could reduce the size or likelihood of harm","Which model is rated highest on public leaderboards, as verified by the supplier"], 2, "MANAGE 2.1 asks that managing risk take into account resources and viable non-AI alternative systems, approaches or methods to reduce the magnitude or likelihood of impacts."),
    S("fr012", "frame", "The business needs something in users' hands within six weeks. A rule-based version can ship in four weeks, while an AI version needs about five months. What is the best decision?", ["Ship the rule-based version in the window and revisit AI later if evidence supports it","Wait for the AI version, however long it takes, and review the plan each month with the sponsor","Ship a rushed AI version in the window and test it live with real users","Cancel the work until an AI version is feasible, then assess the business need again"], 0, "When speed to market matters most, a simpler version can deliver value now. PAIR lists speed and low cost as a case where AI may not be the better choice. Evidence can justify AI later."),
    S("fr013", "frame", "A team says the new search feature needs AI because it is modern, and has compared no alternative. Which request from the PO is most useful?", ["Ask the team to write up the AI vision for the sponsor","Ask for a list of AI features other products released this year","Ask engineering to start training a model in parallel and test it early","Ask what value AI adds beyond a simple rule-based baseline, shown with evidence"], 3, "This is general practice, in line with PAIR: question whether AI will improve the experience and compare against a simple baseline before committing."),
    S("fr014", "frame", "Staff copy figures between two systems for six hours a day. They find it boring, and they agree on the correct method. What does PAIR suggest?", ["Augment it, so staff can take pride in each figure and review it themselves","Automate it, and give staff a way to check, edit or undo the result","Leave it manual, since staff know the rules and can spot errors","Automate it fully and remove the manual route, since the method is clear"], 1, "Tedious, repetitive tasks with an agreed correct method are good automation candidates. PAIR adds that even automated tasks should almost always allow human oversight and intervention."),
    S("fr015", "frame", "A team wants AI to write the notes a counsellor sends to clients, about 60 notes a week per counsellor. Counsellors say the personal tone is the heart of their work. What is the best direction?", ["Automate the notes and monitor the client complaints that follow","Automate the notes sent outside office hours first and watch how clients respond","Offer drafts that each counsellor reviews, edits and sends","Automate the notes first, then ask counsellors to adapt and agree to the change"], 2, "People prefer control when a task carries personal responsibility, high stakes or personal value. Augmentation supports the person without removing their role."),
    S("fr016", "frame", "A team plans to automate approvals of small refunds. Which design choice does PAIR say should almost always be offered?", ["A way for people to oversee and step in, such as preview, review or undo","A promise to stakeholders that the model will be tested until errors are removed","A documented override that sits with the engineering team","A monthly summary report sent to the sponsor and the steering group"], 0, "PAIR says automation should almost always come with an option for human oversight and intervention, for example previewing, testing, editing or undoing."),
    M("fr017", "frame", 2, "Which TWO signs point towards augmenting the person rather than automating the task?", ["The task is boring and repetitive, and staff agree it should go","The task is unsafe for a person to do, so the team checks it carefully","People enjoy the task and want to take part in it","The task is high stakes for the person doing it","People lack the knowledge or skill to do it, and ask for help"], [2,3], "PAIR suggests augmenting when people enjoy the task, carry personal responsibility, face high stakes or find preferences hard to put into words. Boring, dangerous or beyond-skill tasks favour automation."),
    S("fr018", "frame", "About 15 of the 20 interviewed staff say they do not want a particular task automated or helped by AI, but the sponsor wants to proceed. What is the best response?", ["Proceed with the automation and review staff adoption after launch","Proceed but keep the AI out of view of the staff directly involved, and monitor the effects on them","Drop the sponsor's goal and ask staff for new ideas, then pick a plan","Leave that task manual in the first release and apply the AI to steps staff want help with"], 3, "PAIR notes that if people explicitly say they do not want a task automated or augmented with AI, that is a good task not to try to disrupt. Narrowing the release to the steps people want helped respects that signal and still moves toward the sponsor's goal."),
    S("fr019", "frame", "A team wants to fully automate a task in which the law may require a named person to take responsibility. The pilot starts in three weeks. What is the best next step?", ["Automate the task for the pilot and record a named person against each outcome afterwards","Take the question to legal and compliance specialists before choosing the level of automation","Ask the data science team to confirm the rule and document the finding","Follow the sponsor's reading of the requirement and see how the pilot goes"], 1, "PAIR notes that personal responsibility can be required or important for a task. Whether a law requires it is a legal question, and as practical judgement it is not for the PO to settle alone. The right move is to involve the people with legal expertise."),
    S("fr020", "frame", "In a design workshop a data scientist says the team must agree the reward function. In PAIR terms, what does the reward function of an AI model define?", ["How the product is priced and packaged for different customer groups","How many users the feature is expected to reach in the first year","How the model tells right from wrong outputs, which shapes what it tries to optimise","How the team is rewarded if the launch meets its targets"], 2, "The reward function, also called objective or loss function, is how a model determines right and wrong predictions and so what behaviour it optimises. It is a major driver of the user experience."),
    S("fr021", "frame", "Three data scientists have drafted a reward function for a loan-queue tool and ask who else should help shape it. According to PAIR, who should?", ["A cross-functional group that includes at least UX, product and engineering","The data scientists, who know the maths best, with a review by the sponsor","The sponsor, who owns the budget, with the team reading the draft","The vendor supplying the model, since its default settings are widely reviewed"], 0, "PAIR says defining success is a collaborative process across disciplines, with UX, product and engineering perspectives at a minimum, to reveal pitfalls where the function optimises the wrong outcome."),
    S("fr022", "frame", "A tool will flag urgent safety messages for staff. Staff can handle about 100 flags a day, and a missed urgent message can mean harm. Which approach to errors is best?", ["Weigh false alarms and misses equally as the default, and check the result with staff later","Minimise false alarms to keep staff comfortable, and monitor the complaints","Let the model's training process pick the balance, then accept its output","Agree with staff which error is worse, then weight a missed urgent message more heavily"], 3, "PAIR warns that weighing errors equally by default rarely matches real consequences. The cost of a miss and of a false alarm should be discussed and weighted deliberately."),
    S("fr023", "frame", "A team's only success measure is minutes spent in the app. Minutes per user are up 30% since launch, and support contacts have risen too. What is the best challenge from the PO?", ["Accept the measure, since more minutes means more value, and watch the trend","Ask what would happen to users over time if the measure were perfectly optimised","Add a second activity count, such as taps per session, to cross-check it","Replace it with a count of new sign-ups each quarter"], 1, "PAIR warns that simple, narrow, immediate measures can have bad long-term effects, and suggests asking what happens to users and society if the function were perfectly optimised."),
    M("fr024", "frame", 2, "Which TWO belong in a team's agreed statement of success and failure for an AI feature?", ["A specific metric with a meaningful threshold","The name of the model, once engineering has tested it","The action the team will take if the threshold is crossed","The launch date announced to stakeholders","A list of the vendors that procurement has reviewed"], [0,2], "PAIR suggests a simple statement: if a specific metric for the feature crosses a meaningful threshold, we will take a specific action. That keeps everyone aligned on success, failure and who acts."),
    S("fr025", "frame", "After launch, a team finds the feature's error rate is three times higher for one user group, about 8% of users, while the overall average looks healthy. What best fits PAIR's guidance on monitoring negative impact?", ["Note it as an edge case and look at it in the next planning round","Wait for the overall average to drop, then assess whether to act","Talk with that group, find the cause, and adapt the product","Report the group's results as a separate line in the next quarterly report and keep the release as it is"], 2, "PAIR advises checking impact metrics on a schedule and, when some stakeholders are harmed, talking to them and adapting the product. It also asks teams to assess inclusivity."),
    S("fr026", "frame", "A PO is drafting the context section of a framing document. Which set of items best matches what MAP 1.1 asks teams to understand and document about a system's context?", ["Intended purposes, users and their expectations, and possible positive and negative impacts","Model type, supplier, hosting region, release cadence and licence terms","Budget, team members, sprint dates, delivery risks and the release plan","Marketing message, price band, target revenue and launch campaign dates"], 0, "MAP 1.1 covers intended purposes, beneficial uses, the types of users and expectations, potential positive and negative impacts, and assumptions and limitations. The other lists are about delivery."),
    M("fr027", "frame", 2, "Which TWO should be written down before building an AI feature, in line with MAP 3.1 and 3.2?", ["The final choice of model settings, once engineering has tested them","The potential benefits of the intended functionality","The names of everyone who attended a workshop","The potential costs of expected or actual errors","The tool used to track tasks"], [1,3], "MAP 3.1 asks for benefits to be examined and documented, and MAP 3.2 asks the same for costs of errors, including non-monetary costs. Settings, attendance and tools are not part of that framing."),
    S("fr028", "frame", "An AI feature launched two years ago costs the team about a fifth of a sprint in upkeep, and nobody can say what business value it delivers. Which action fits MAP 1.4?", ["Leave it as it is, because no complaints have come in and the team has reviewed it","Expand it to more users and teams, so the value shows up in the usage figures","Add more training data to improve it, then test whether usage changes","Define the business value and re-evaluate the context of use, then decide whether to continue"], 3, "MAP 1.4 asks that business value be clearly defined or, for an existing system, re-evaluated. Without a known value, continuing or expanding is a guess."),
    S("fr029", "frame", "A PO believes errors from a planned feature could seriously harm users, but the organisation has not said how much risk it will accept. The build starts next sprint. What is the best action?", ["Set a tolerance yourself and document it in the product backlog today","Escalate to the people accountable for risk with the evidence and agree a tolerance","Start the build and look at the risk once the first release is out","Ask engineers to define an acceptable error rate and check it in testing before the build"], 1, "MAP 3.2 links costs of errors to organisational risk tolerance, MAP 1.5 asks that tolerances be determined and documented, and GOVERN 2.3 says executive leadership takes responsibility for risk decisions. As practical judgement, the PO supplies evidence and escalates."),
    S("fr030", "frame", "A draft business case for a triage assistant lists benefits in detail but says nothing about what happens when the system is wrong. The steering meeting is on Friday. What is the best response?", ["Approve it, since errors can be found and handled in testing before the pilot ends","Approve it, since the listed benefits are well evidenced and clearly outweigh the costs","Add the costs of errors, including non-monetary costs, before approval","Ask the team to rewrite the benefits section to make it more convincing to the committee"], 2, "MAP 3.1 and 3.2 ask for both benefits and costs to be examined. A case with only benefits cannot show that the trade-off is worthwhile."),
    S("fr031", "frame", "A PO is writing the scope statement for a document-summary assistant. According to MAP 3.3, what should the targeted application scope be based on?", ["The system's capability, its established context and how it is categorised","The most ambitious ask stakeholders have documented","The size of the remaining budget","The scope used by the nearest competitor"], 0, "MAP 3.3 asks that scope be specified and documented based on capability, established context and AI system categorisation."),
    S("fr032", "frame", "A team wants to offer an assistant in a second language that was never tested. The model is the same, and 12% of users read that language. What is the best decision?", ["Include it, since the model is the same, and monitor the results for a few weeks","Include it with a notice that results may vary, and ask users to report problems","Include it in the pilot, and have bilingual staff sample the outputs once a month","Keep that language out of scope until it has been assessed on real examples"], 3, "MAP 3.3 asks that scope be specified and documented from capability and established context. As practical judgement, results in tested conditions do not carry over automatically, so an untested language stays out of scope until it has been evaluated."),
    S("fr033", "frame", "A PO is writing the scope for an internal help assistant. Why is it useful to state what the assistant will NOT do, as well as what it will do?", ["It means requests outside the stated scope need no testing","It sets clear limits so users and teams know when to look elsewhere","It removes the need for a human fallback, since the limits are written down","It helps the product look more capable to sponsors and buyers"], 1, "A clear out-of-scope statement helps users and teams know where the system can be relied on. It does not remove the need for testing within scope or for fallback routes."),
    S("fr034", "frame", "A stakeholder asks to extend a working internal assistant to answer health questions from staff. It is live for 400 staff on HR policy. What is the best response?", ["Switch it on, since the assistant is already live, and monitor the questions each week","Add a disclaimer, extend the scope and read the first month of answers","Treat it as a new use: frame it, involve specialists, keep it out of scope until then","Let staff try it as a pilot and check the complaints that come in"], 2, "A new use has new users, impacts and costs of error. It needs its own framing and the right expertise, so it stays out of the current scope until that is done."),
    S("fr035", "frame", "What should the PO do first, before choosing a solution?", ["Talk to front-desk staff and patients to find why visits are missed","Train a prediction model on last year's booking export, then plan the double-booking rules","Ask the vendor shortlist for demos of no-show prediction and review their claims","Write requirements for automatic double-booking with the scheduling lead"], 0, "The sponsor has proposed a solution without evidence for the cause. Staff say misses come from forgetting, so gathering evidence about causes comes before any solution.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr036", "frame", "Staff believe most misses are simple forgetting. Which option should be tested first?", ["A prediction model that is retuned weekly and checked against new bookings","A chatbot that lets patients rebook or cancel their own slots by message","A redesigned booking page, monitored after launch, that offers personalised slots","Timely text or phone reminders, tested in a small trial against the letters"], 3, "If forgetting is the cause, a simple reminder may fix it more cheaply and with fewer risks. As practical judgement, test a non-AI option against the current baseline first.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr037", "frame", "The team does build a model that predicts likely misses. A wasted slot costs the clinic, while a double-booked attendee may wait an hour. How should it decide the cost of each kind of error?", ["By weighing both errors equally as the default, and looking at the result after launch","With clinicians and patients, weighing a wasted slot against a double-booked attendee","By letting the sponsor choose the cheaper error and documenting the choice","By following the vendor's recommended settings, which suit most clinics"], 1, "The two errors have different consequences, one for efficiency and one for patient care. People who understand both sides should weigh them in the success definition.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr038", "frame", "Should the system double-book slots automatically, or show staff the risk and let them decide?", ["Double-book automatically, since it saves staff time and the model has been tested","Double-book automatically for patients the model flags as high risk, and watch results","Show staff the risk score and let them decide each booking","Double-book automatically on the busiest days of the week and track the waits"], 2, "The stakes for patients are high and staff carry responsibility. Augmentation keeps a person deciding and can later expand if evidence supports it.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr039", "frame", "The booking export contains health-related details. What is the right next step?", ["Involve the privacy team before using that data for a prediction model","Use the data now and tell the privacy team at launch, with a review afterwards","Remove the patient names from the export, continue the model build and see how it performs","Ask the data team to decide whether the export is acceptable and document why"], 0, "Health-related personal data is potentially regulated. The PO does not give legal or privacy rulings and should involve the privacy team and compliance specialists early.", "scFR1"),
    M("fr040", "frame", 2, "Which TWO should the team write into the scope of the first release?", ["Which appointment types are included","The vendor that supplied the data export","The names of staff who attended workshops","A promise to extend to the other clinics later, once agreed","Which clinics have evidence behind the results"], [0,4], "A scope statement follows what the system can do and the context where it was shown to work. Appointment types and evidence-backed locations are scope. Vendors, attendees and promises are not.", "scFR1"),
    S("fr041", "frame", "About half of the questions are the same dozen policy questions with fixed answers, and the policy wording changes a few times a year. Which approach should be tried first for these?", ["A model that writes a fresh answer to each question, with finance reviewing a sample","A model that learns from past tickets, tested monthly and retrained when the policy changes","A voice interface that reads the policy aloud to employees who ask","A maintained FAQ or rules drawn from the current policy and checked by finance"], 3, "PAIR notes that a rule or simple solution is often easier to build, explain and maintain than an AI one. As practical judgement, fixed, repeated questions are the best fit for it, and a maintained source also stays correct when policy wording changes.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr042", "frame", "The sponsor's measure is 'fewer tickets'. Which success measure is best?", ["Number of questions the assistant receives, reviewed weekly with the sponsor","Correct answers and users who need no repeat contact, agreed with finance","Number of tickets closed per support agent, checked monthly","Minutes users spend chatting with the assistant"], 1, "Fewer tickets can be achieved by unhelpful answers. A success measure should reflect correct, resolved answers and be agreed by the people involved.", "scFR2"),
    M("fr043", "frame", 2, "Which TWO are non-monetary costs of wrong answers from the assistant?", ["The licence fee for the assistant","Extra finance staff hours to correct claims","Employees lose trust in the help channel","The hosting cost of the pilot, once IT has reviewed it","Employees feel stress when claims are rejected"], [2,4], "MAP 3.2 asks for non-monetary costs of errors to be examined. Trust and stress are real costs without a price tag. Fees, hours and hosting are monetary.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr044", "frame", "The other half of the questions are unusual cases that need a judgement by the finance team, roughly 30 a week. What is the best design?", ["Let the assistant decide and have finance check a sample each month","Let the assistant decide and have employees appeal through the help desk","Keep finance staff as decision makers and have the assistant prepare the facts","Stop accepting unusual cases through the assistant, so users get no answer until finance has capacity"], 2, "Judgement cases are high in cost of error and hard to put into rules, so augmentation fits: the assistant gathers facts and a specialist decides.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr045", "frame", "What should the written scope say about the assistant's limits?", ["It answers from the current policy and routes other questions to finance specialists","It answers expense questions from policy and general knowledge, and staff check the gaps","It answers policy questions and offers its own advice on how exceptions are handled, with managers asked to review it","It answers from any source the model rates as helpful, including the public web"], 0, "A scope based on what the system can do reliably, with a route to people for the rest, protects users and keeps the system within its tested conditions.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr046", "frame", "A manager asks the assistant to also advise on the tax treatment of expenses. What is the best response?", ["Add it, since the assistant already handles expenses, and review complaints monthly","Add it with a note that advice is general, and test it on sample claims","Add it as a trial for one team and monitor the questions it receives","Involve finance, tax and legal specialists and keep tax advice out of scope until they agree"], 3, "Tax treatment is specialist territory and the PO cannot settle it alone. A new use goes through its own framing with the right experts, and stays out of scope until then.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr047", "frame", "The tested FAQ for the dozen policy questions is ready, but routing of unusual cases to finance needs two more weeks. The sponsor wants the ticket target addressed this quarter. What should the PO do?", ["Hold the whole release until the unusual-case routing is built","Ship the FAQ part now, keep the current route to finance for unusual cases, and build routing next","Release the assistant for unusual cases too, and watch the rejected claims","Ask finance to check each answer before release, then ship the complete assistant to the whole company"], 1, "A partial release covers the fixed questions that are already tested and keeps the current route for the rest, so value starts early without answering cases the team has not framed. This is general practice on narrowing a release.", "scFR2"),
    S("fr048", "frame", "A pilot of a drafting assistant for internal reports shows 2% of drafts contain a minor factual slip. Each draft is edited and signed by its author before it leaves the team, and the risk owner has been told the figure and the control and has raised no objection. What should the PO do?", ["Stop the pilot until the slip rate reaches zero, then test again","Escalate each slip found to the executive committee for a ruling","Proceed, keep author sign-off, and report the slip rate to the accountable risk owner each sprint","Proceed and drop the slip-rate figure, since the risk owner has been told the level once and the control exists"], 2, "A small, caught error rate with a human control and the accountable person informed is a residual risk for the accountable risk owner to accept or not; the PO does not accept it alone, and keeps reporting the figure so the owner's view stays evidence-based. Zero errors is not a realistic bar, and dropping the measurement removes the evidence the risk owner relies on. This is general practice consistent with GOVERN 2.3."),
    S("fr049", "frame", "A sponsor asks the PO to add automatic rejection of low-scored job applications to a pilot that was framed for ranking candidates for recruiters to review. No evaluation exists for rejection, and the pilot starts in two weeks. What should the PO do?", ["Decline the addition for now, keep recruiters deciding, and log it for HR, legal and privacy assessment","Add rejection to the pilot for all low scores and have recruiters look at the rejected group afterwards","Add rejection for the lowest-scored 5% and check the outcomes against recruiter decisions","Pause the pilot and escalate the sponsor's request to the board before any further work starts"], 0, "The pilot was framed and scoped for ranking with a person deciding. Rejection is a new use with its own costs of error, so it stays out of scope until framed and evaluated. Pausing everything over a scope request is an over-reaction. Hiring decisions can also carry legal and privacy duties, so the request goes to HR, legal and privacy specialists for assessment before any extension."),
    S("fr050", "frame", "A rule-based triage for IT tickets routes 70% correctly. An AI version routes 82% correctly in testing on last quarter's tickets, which have a similar mix. Misroutes cost a day's delay, not harm. What is the best decision?", ["Hold the release until the AI version scores above 95% in testing on the same tickets","Replace the rule version and retire it, since the AI score is higher","Keep the rule version and ask engineering to collect another quarter of test data first","Release the AI version to a pilot group with an agreed switch-off rule, keeping the rule version as fallback"], 3, "The errors are low in cost and reversible, and testing used a similar mix of tickets, so a controlled pilot with a switch-off rule and a fallback is a defensible decision now. Retiring the old version removes the safety net, and further waiting delays learning from real use. This is general practice.")
  );

  Object.assign(window.AIPO.why, {
    "fr001": ["This starts from the technology and optimises for a demo, not for a need.","A sized backlog of model features assumes AI is the answer before any problem is confirmed.","Understanding the problem and today's workaround is the foundation for any later choice, AI or not.","Choosing a supplier commits to a solution before the problem is understood."],
    "fr002": ["It starts from the problem and asks for AI's unique contribution, which is the PAIR framing.","This is the technology-first form that PAIR advises moving away from.","Popularity of an approach says nothing about whether users need it.","Copying a rival begins with their solution, not with your users' problem."],
    "fr003": ["A solution is chosen before the problem is known, and AI is only one guess.","This acts on one view, which may be wrong or may miss the real cause, and checks only after release.","Recording an unevidenced claim as confirmed makes an opinion look like a fact.","A short stretch of observation and data work turns an opinion into a defined problem and may change which fix is right."],
    "fr004": ["Mapping is neutral. It may well show that a simpler fix is enough.","The map exposes real pain points and candidate steps, whatever solution follows.","A process map is not a dataset, and data needs are a separate question.","Mapping works best alongside conversations and observation, not instead of them."],
    "fr005": ["Observing real use shows needs and workarounds that people may not describe.","Competitor features show solutions, not your users' problems.","Engineer preferences are about techniques, not user needs.","Data on current behaviour complements what people say and do.","Requirements and metrics for a model before user contact assume the solution."],
    "fr006": ["Building first spends the most effort before learning whether people accept the idea.","Stakeholder guesses are not the reactions of the people who will use it.","A human-run simulation is cheap and shows real reactions before any build cost (general practice).","A live release learns from complaints after the effect has reached staff, and the build cost is already spent."],
    "fr007": ["A rule does the job and is simpler to explain and maintain, which is the better product choice.","A fixed format has few edge cases a rule cannot handle, so the gain is small.","Team learning is a poor reason for the user to take on extra cost and complexity.","A comparison skips the prior question of whether AI is needed at all."],
    "fr008": ["Static information is the textbook case where a simple solution wins.","Predictability is the value here, which AI can weaken.","A fixed table is deterministic, so a rule is simpler and easier to explain.","Evolving, rare patterns are hard to capture in fixed rules, so learning from data adds unique value."],
    "fr009": ["A higher average hides the severe cost of the rare bad outcome.","A small gain does not justify severe error costs, so the simpler approach is better.","Learning from harm after launch accepts the cost on users who did not choose it.","A slight rise in success does not remove the severity of the remaining errors."],
    "fr010": ["Personalisation is a typical case where AI adds unique value.","AI cannot always give a full account of every step, so a rule suits this need.","Understanding varied language is hard to cover with rules.","Recognising a whole class cannot be programmed item by item.","Predictable behaviour is easier to guarantee with fixed logic."],
    "fr011": ["A rival's launch is a market signal, not a risk-reducing alternative.","Effort already spent is not a reason to keep an approach.","The subcategory names non-AI alternatives as part of the response to risk.","Leaderboard rank is unrelated to whether AI is the right approach."],
    "fr012": ["A simple version delivers value on time and keeps the AI option open.","Delay gives up the value of the time-critical need.","A rushed AI release tests the riskiest option on real users.","Stopping removes value users could already have."],
    "fr013": ["A vision statement repeats the claim without testing it.","Other products' features do not show value for your users.","Training a model first commits effort before the need is shown.","A baseline comparison tests whether AI is really needed."],
    "fr014": ["Staff find no enjoyment or social value in this task, so augmentation adds little.","It matches the PAIR signs for automation and keeps a route for oversight.","A boring task with agreed rules is the classic candidate for automation, and monitoring errors by hand wastes it.","Even automated tasks should allow people to step in."],
    "fr015": ["Complaints arrive after harm to the relationship and trust.","Partial automation still removes the counsellor's role in some cases.","It respects the personal responsibility and meaning of the task while still giving help.","Asking for adaptation after the fact reverses the sensible order."],
    "fr016": ["Oversight options let people catch and correct errors in real time.","No AI can promise zero errors, and such a claim sets false expectations.","A control held by engineers gives affected users no say.","A report to the sponsor is too slow to prevent harm to users."],
    "fr017": ["Tedious work is a typical automation candidate.","Risky work is better handed to a machine where possible.","Taking the task away would remove what people value about it.","People often want to stay in control when the stakes are high for them.","A machine can fill a skill gap people cannot close."],
    "fr018": ["Adoption problems are predictable if the people affected were not heard.","Hiding the system undermines trust and transparency.","Dropping the goal outright skips the chance to meet it another way.","It takes the signal seriously and still delivers help where staff want it."],
    "fr019": ["Naming a person afterwards does not meet an obligation that applies to the process.","Legal questions go to the people qualified to answer them, before design is fixed.","Data science is not the right specialism for a legal requirement.","A sponsor's reading is not an expert view of the legal position."],
    "fr020": ["Pricing is a business decision, not the model's objective.","Reach is a business target, not a model objective.","It defines success and failure for the model, steering its behaviour.","The term is not about team incentives."],
    "fr021": ["Different disciplines spot different pitfalls, which is the PAIR recommendation.","Technical skill misses user and product consequences, and a late review does not fix that.","One person cannot see the user, technical and product effects.","Defaults are not tailored to your users, and the choice is not the vendor's to make."],
    "fr022": ["Equal weights are a tempting default that PAIR warns against.","Reducing false alarms may raise missed urgent messages.","The balance is a product decision, not a by-product of training.","Real consequences differ, so the weights should reflect them."],
    "fr023": ["Time spent can rise for bad reasons, such as distraction or friction.","It tests second-order effects, which is the PAIR check for a narrow measure.","Two activity counts share the same weakness.","Sign-ups are a different activity count and say nothing about benefit."],
    "fr024": ["A measurable threshold makes success and failure clear.","The model name is an implementation detail.","A named action turns the threshold into a decision.","A date is a schedule item, not a success statement.","Vendors considered are background, not a measure of success."],
    "fr025": ["Dismissing a group as an edge case leaves harm in place.","An average can stay healthy while one group is poorly served.","It listens to those affected and acts, as PAIR recommends.","A separate report line records the gap but leaves the harm in place for another quarter."],
    "fr026": ["These are core items in the MAP 1.1 description.","These are build details, not context of use.","These are delivery facts, not the intended use and impacts.","These are commercial facts, not the context of use."],
    "fr027": ["Settings are chosen later and are not a framing decision.","Benefits must be examined and documented, not assumed.","Attendance is administrative.","Costs of errors need the same attention as benefits.","A tracking tool is a delivery detail."],
    "fr028": ["No complaints is not the same as demonstrated value.","Expanding a feature of unknown value scales the uncertainty.","More data does not tell you what value is being delivered.","It restates the value and context so the next decision is based on evidence."],
    "fr029": ["The PO does not own the risk decision alone.","As good practice, tolerance is agreed with those accountable for risk before building, not set by the PO alone.","Reviewing after release puts users at risk first.","Engineers can inform the rate but do not own the risk decision."],
    "fr030": ["Testing finds errors but does not weigh what they cost.","Without costs, nobody can show benefits outweigh them.","A balanced view of benefits and costs is needed to judge the trade-off.","Stronger wording does not fix a missing analysis."],
    "fr031": ["Scope should follow what the system can do and the context it is used in.","Ambition is not evidence of capability.","Budget affects planning but is not the basis the subcategory names.","A competitor's scope does not fit your system or context."],
    "fr032": ["The same model does not guarantee the same quality in a new language.","A notice shifts the risk to users without evidence.","Monthly sampling means the first wrong answers reach real users before anyone checks.","As practical judgement, scope should reflect what has been shown to work; the language can be added after evaluation."],
    "fr033": ["Testing within scope is still needed, and edge behaviour still occurs.","Defined limits help people know where to go for other needs.","Users still need a route when the system cannot help.","The aim is honesty about limits, not perceived capability."],
    "fr034": ["Being live in one context does not make it suited to another.","A disclaimer does not address the harm of wrong answers.","A new use deserves its own framing and expert input before it is in scope.","Complaints are late evidence for a high-stakes topic."],
    "fr035": ["Evidence about causes shapes which solution, AI or not, is worth trying.","Training starts from a solution and from data that may not hold the cause.","Demos show products, not the cause of the problem.","Requirements for double-booking lock in a solution without evidence."],
    "fr036": ["A prediction model does not remove forgetting and adds cost and risk.","A chatbot adds a new interaction, not a reminder.","A new booking page does not address the cause.","A simple non-AI change addresses the stated cause and is easy to measure."],
    "fr037": ["Equal weighting is a default that rarely fits real consequences.","The people who live with each error help set the weights.","Cost to the clinic is only part of the picture, and one voice is not enough.","Vendor settings are not tailored to this clinic's patients."],
    "fr038": ["Time saved does not outweigh patient harm from wrong double-bookings.","A high-risk flag is still a prediction that can be wrong.","Augmenting keeps accountable humans in the decision where patient impact is high.","Busy days raise the cost of a wrong double-booking."],
    "fr039": ["The privacy team is the right specialist, and early involvement avoids rework and harm.","Telling them at launch is too late to change the design.","Removing names may not address the privacy question, and the result check does not settle it.","A data team is not the privacy authority."],
    "fr040": ["Naming included appointment types sets clear limits.","A data source is not a scope boundary.","Attendance is administrative.","A promise to expand is a plan, not scope based on evidence.","Tying scope to clinics with evidence reflects what is known to work."],
    "fr041": ["Fresh generation adds variation where fixed answers are wanted.","Learning from tickets may repeat outdated answers when policy changes.","A voice layer changes the interface, not the quality of answers.","A simple maintained source suits fixed answers and is easy to update."],
    "fr042": ["Volume of questions shows use, not help.","It tests whether users got right answers, and it is defined across functions.","Closure counts reflect support activity, not user outcomes.","Long chats may signal confusion, not value."],
    "fr043": ["A fee is a monetary cost.","Staff hours can be priced.","Loss of trust is a lasting non-monetary cost.","Hosting is a monetary cost.","Stress and friction for people are non-monetary costs."],
    "fr044": ["Sampling finds errors after the harm has occurred.","Appeals put the burden on employees after a wrong decision.","It keeps accountable specialists deciding and still saves their time.","Users get no answer in the meantime, which removes value they need."],
    "fr045": ["Limited scope with a handover is honest and safe.","General knowledge stretches beyond tested conditions, and feedback arrives after wrong answers.","Advice on exceptions needs finance judgement, not the assistant.","Uncontrolled sources risk wrong or outdated answers."],
    "fr046": ["Being related to expenses does not make it safe to extend.","A general note does not address wrong answers, and sample claims do not cover tax rules.","A small trial still exposes real claims to untested tax advice.","The right specialists must frame the use and its limits before it is in scope."],
    "fr047": ["Holding everything delays value that the tested part could deliver now.","It delivers the tested part early and keeps the current route for the cases not yet ready.","Unusual cases need finance judgement, and answering them without routing risks rejected claims.","Checking each answer adds delay and still ships a scope that has not been framed."],
    "fr048": ["Zero errors is not a realistic bar, and stopping loses a pilot that is already controlled.","Sending each minor slip to executives is process theatre and buries the real decisions.","The control stays, the risk owner stays informed and the product decision remains the PO's.","Dropping the measurement removes the evidence that the level the risk owner was told still holds."],
    "fr049": ["The pilot was framed for ranking with a person deciding, so a new use stays out until framed and evaluated, and HR, legal and privacy assess the request.","Reviewing after rejection puts the harm to candidates before the check.","A small slice is still a new use with no evaluation behind it.","Stopping the whole pilot over a scope request is an over-reaction, and the board need not rule on it."],
    "fr050": ["An arbitrary bar delays learning on a low-cost, reversible decision.","Retiring the rule version removes the fallback and a test score is not live evidence.","More data work postpones the decision without a trigger for when it would be enough.","A pilot with a switch-off rule and a fallback limits the cost of errors while real evidence builds."]
  });

  window.AIPO.cards.push(
    { id: "frc01", domain: "frame", front: "What question should replace 'Can we use AI to ...?'", back: "'How might we solve this problem?', followed by 'Can AI solve it in a unique way?'\nTrap: starting from the technology, so every option except AI is ignored." },
    { id: "frc02", domain: "frame", front: "Name three ways to find the real user problem.", back: "Talk to people, observe their behaviour and look through data.\nTrap: relying on one stakeholder's opinion." },
    { id: "frc03", domain: "frame", front: "What is a Wizard of Oz test?", back: "A test where a person quietly performs the automated step so you see how users react before building. PAIR names the test; this description is the common usage.\nTrap: building the full model first and asking about acceptance afterwards." },
    { id: "frc04", domain: "frame", front: "What do MAP 1.1 and MAP 1.4 ask the team to establish?", back: "Intended purposes, users, expectations and impacts (1.1), and a clearly defined or re-evaluated business value (1.4).\nTrap: assuming the value of an old feature is still known." },
    { id: "frc05", domain: "frame", front: "Why is a rule or heuristic often a good answer?", back: "It is usually easier to build, explain, debug and maintain, and it may work as well as AI.\nTrap: treating 'no AI' as failure." },
    { id: "frc06", domain: "frame", front: "Give two situations where AI is probably the better fit.", back: "Personalised recommendations, predicting events, natural language, recognising a whole class, or rare changing events such as fraud.\nTrap: applying the list to tasks where predictability matters." },
    { id: "frc07", domain: "frame", front: "Give two situations where AI is probably not better.", back: "Predictability is the value, information is static, costly errors outweigh a small gain, or full transparency is required.\nTrap: accepting a small average gain despite severe rare errors." },
    { id: "frc08", domain: "frame", front: "What does MANAGE 2.1 ask about alternatives?", back: "That viable non-AI alternatives are considered along with resources when managing risk.\nTrap: comparing AI options only with each other." },
    { id: "frc09", domain: "frame", front: "When is automation usually preferred?", back: "When the task is boring, repetitive, awkward or dangerous, people lack the skill, or people agree on the correct method.\nTrap: automating without any way for people to oversee or undo." },
    { id: "frc10", domain: "frame", front: "When is augmentation usually preferred?", back: "When people enjoy the task, carry personal responsibility, face high stakes or find preferences hard to describe.\nTrap: automating a task that people value doing." },
    { id: "frc11", domain: "frame", front: "What should almost always be offered even for automated tasks?", back: "A way for people to oversee and step in, such as preview, edit or undo.\nTrap: giving control only to engineers." },
    { id: "frc12", domain: "frame", front: "Staff say they do not want a task helped by AI. What next?", back: "Treat it as a signal not to disrupt that task: explore why and look for other ways to meet the goal.\nTrap: proceeding and hoping they adapt." },
    { id: "frc13", domain: "frame", front: "What is the reward function in PAIR terms?", back: "How an AI model tells right from wrong outputs, which shapes what it optimises.\nTrap: confusing it with team incentives or business targets." },
    { id: "frc14", domain: "frame", front: "What is the difference between a false positive and a false negative?", back: "A false positive is a wrong 'yes' such as a false alarm. A false negative is a wrong 'no' such as a missed case.\nTrap: weighing both equally by default." },
    { id: "frc15", domain: "frame", front: "Why test the precision and recall balance with users?", back: "The two trade off and there is no way around it, so users help find the right point.\nTrap: demanding the maximum of both." },
    { id: "frc16", domain: "frame", front: "What form does PAIR suggest for a success and failure statement?", back: "If a specific metric crosses a meaningful threshold, we will take a specific action.\nTrap: a metric with no threshold or no action." },
    { id: "frc17", domain: "frame", front: "What do MAP 3.1 and MAP 3.2 ask to be documented?", back: "Potential benefits (3.1) and potential costs of errors, including non-monetary costs, linked to risk tolerance (3.2).\nTrap: a business case with benefits only." },
    { id: "frc18", domain: "frame", front: "Give examples of non-monetary costs of AI errors.", back: "Lost trust, distress, unfair treatment or harm to a person.\nTrap: counting only fees, hosting and staff time." },
    { id: "frc19", domain: "frame", front: "Who decides risk tolerance?", back: "MAP 1.5 asks that tolerances are determined and documented; GOVERN 2.3 says executive leadership takes responsibility for AI risk decisions. As judgement, the PO supplies evidence and escalates.\nTrap: the PO setting it alone." },
    { id: "frc20", domain: "frame", front: "What does MAP 3.3 ask about scope?", back: "That targeted application scope is specified and documented from capability, context and categorisation.\nTrap: letting scope drift to untested users or tasks." }
  );

  window.AIPO.lessons.push(
    {
      "id": "frame-6",
      "n": 6,
      "domain": "frame",
      "title": "Just enough technology: what each way of building changes",
      "body": "Four ways of building an AI feature change a Product Owner's decisions. These definitions are general practice, not from a source. Prompting means steering a general model with written instructions and examples; it is cheap to change, needs little data of your own, and is evaluated by running realistic cases. Retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, means the system looks up documents or records when a question arrives and gives them to the model; you must hold those documents, have the right to use them and keep them current, and evaluation covers both the lookup and the answer.\n\nFine-tuning means training a model further on your own examples. It needs good examples and specialist effort, and training creates no right to use them. Agents are systems that can take actions, not only write text. Their mistakes become actions, so tests must cover what they do, and a person should approve steps that are hard to undo.\n\nNIST AI 600-1 asks that such models be re-evaluated (MP-4.1-007), that fine-tuning be verified not to weaken safety and security controls (MS-2.7-008), and that training and test data provenance be verified and fine-tuning or RAG data be grounded (MS-2.5-005). Extending this to a plain model switch is practical judgement. NIST also describes indirect prompt injection, instructions hidden in retrieved data; as practical judgement, a written instruction alone does not close it, so limit what it can do. Ask the team: what is the model given, where does the data come from, what can it do without a person, and how do we test after a change?",
      "points": [
        "Prompting steers a general model with instructions and examples: cheapest to change, so test each change.",
        "RAG hands retrieved documents to the model: you need rights to the data, a way to keep it current, and tests of lookup and answer.",
        "Fine-tuning trains on your examples: confirm you may use them (general practice), and re-evaluate afterwards (MP-4.1-007, MS-2.7-008, MS-2.5-005).",
        "Agents can act, not only write: set what they may do alone and where a person approves.",
        "Ask what the model is given, where the data comes from, what it can do alone and how a change is tested."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Believing that fine-tuning settles a question about the right to use the data.",
        "Testing the final answer on the old test set after the documents or the model changed.",
        "Giving an agent the power to act before its mistakes and limits are known.",
        "Treating a supplier's demo or a technique's name as evidence of quality."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile (July 2024), action MP-4.1-007",
        "NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile (July 2024), action MS-2.7-008",
        "NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile (July 2024), action MS-2.5-005",
        "NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile (July 2024), section 2.9 Information Security (prompt injection)",
        "General practice, not from a source: the plain definitions of prompting, RAG, fine-tuning and agents, and the cost, data and oversight comparisons"
      ],
      "sourceVersion": "NIST AI 600-1, July 2024",
      "reviewed": false
    }
  );

  window.AIPO.questions.push(
    S("fr051", "frame", "A team proposes adding retrieval to an internal help assistant so it answers from 1,200 policy pages. The pages sit in three shared folders, and about a third have no named owner. What should the PO ask for first?", ["A speed test of the assistant during the busiest hour, reviewed with IT before the pilot","A comparison of three retrieval settings on sample questions, with results documented for the team","A record of which pages it may read, who owns each set and how stale pages are found and removed","A list of the topics the assistant should decline, agreed with the sponsor and published to staff before launch week"], 2, "Retrieval gives the model whatever the lookup finds, so the PO needs to know the source, its owners and its upkeep. Without owners, stale or unapproved pages can reach users as answers."),
    S("fr052", "frame", "A supplier proposes fine-tuning a model on 40,000 past customer chats to improve its tone. The chats were collected for support, and nobody has confirmed that training on them is allowed. What is the best response?", ["Keep the chats out of training until privacy confirms the permitted use, and try prompt guidance and examples meanwhile","Go ahead, because training changes the model and the chats are not held in a readable form once training is done","Fine-tune on a sample of 4,000 chats first and have the privacy team review the sample results after the test run","Fine-tune after names are removed from the chats, and agree the cleaned set with the data team before release"], 0, "Fine-tuning uses the examples, so it does not create a right to use them. Prompting with guidance and examples lets the work continue while the people responsible for privacy decide on the permitted use."),
    S("fr053", "frame", "A retrieval assistant scores 92% on a fixed set of 150 test questions. The supplier offers a newer underlying model at a lower running cost. The team's agreed threshold is 90%. Which plan best fits?", ["Switch on the strength of the supplier's published benchmark results, then monitor user complaints for two weeks","Re-run the questions that failed before, since the others already passed, and compare the new results with them","Release the newer model to half the users and compare ticket volume and complaint themes by question type","Run the same 150 questions on the new model by question type, and switch if results meet the threshold"], 3, "A changed model is a changed system, so the team's own test set is re-run and read by question type against the agreed threshold. NIST AI 600-1 MP-4.1-007 asks for re-evaluation of models fine-tuned or enhanced on third-party models; applying the same logic to a model switch is practical judgement."),
    S("fr054", "frame", "The finance team will replace 300 of the assistant's 900 source documents with a new policy version next week. The model and instructions stay the same. What should the PO require before the change goes live?", ["A re-run of the questions about the 300 replaced documents, with the results reviewed by the policy owner after release","A re-run of the full test set plus new questions for the changed policy, signed off by the policy owner","A check by IT that the new files load and can be found by the search step, reported to the team before release","A spot check of three answers from the new documents, agreed with the policy owner and logged for review"], 1, "The documents are part of what the model is given, so a change to them can alter answers on topics beyond the replaced pages. The full set, plus new questions for the changed policy, shows both what moved and what broke."),
    S("fr055", "frame", "An agent can read an order, draft a refund and send it to the payment system. A wrong refund costs about 80 units. In 200 test cases it drafted 3 wrong refunds, and the sponsor wants it live next month. What is the best release plan?", ["Let it pay refunds from launch, and have finance sample the payments each week and report the wrong ones","Release it to one region with direct payment, and monitor the wrong payments daily against the agreed limit","Release drafting now with a person approving each refund, and widen direct payment as real results support it","Hold the release until a larger test set shows fewer than 1 wrong draft in 200, then review again with the sponsor and finance owner"], 2, "Drafting with a person approving gets value now while the agent's real error rate is learned. Direct payment can widen in steps as evidence supports it. A near-zero bar is hard to prove on a test set, and sampling after payment leaves wrong refunds already paid."),
    S("fr056", "frame", "A supplier says its product is 'tuned for your industry' and shows a demo of 20 questions. The PO is asked to recommend it for a pilot with 300 staff. Which question to the supplier is most useful?", ["What is the model given at answer time, what was it tuned on, and can we re-run our own tests after updates?","How many other organisations use the product, and can they share their satisfaction scores with our team?","Which security certificates does the company hold, and when were they last reviewed by an outside body, in writing?","How quickly can your team add the features that staff request during the pilot, and at what review points?"], 0, "The PO needs to know what the system is given, what data shaped it and how changes can be tested. A demo, a customer list or a certificate list does not show that."),
    S("fr057", "frame", "An assistant reads supplier emails from an open mailbox and can create purchase orders up to 5,000 units. A tester shows that a line hidden in an email makes it raise an order. What is the best decision?", ["Add an instruction telling the assistant to treat email text as data, not commands, and re-run the earlier tests","Lower the order limit to 500 units and have the buyer check the order log each week for odd orders","Switch the assistant off for the whole company until security has signed off the redesigned approach","Keep it reading emails but remove order creation so a person raises orders, and add hidden-line test cases"], 3, "NIST AI 600-1 (section 2.9) describes indirect prompt injection through data the system retrieves. As practical judgement, a written instruction alone is not a reliable fix, so removing the ability to act limits the harm now while reading continues, and the new test cases keep the weakness visible."),
    M("fr058", "frame", 2, "A team proposes fine-tuning a model on 5,000 labelled examples taken from its own tickets. Which TWO questions should the PO ask?", ["Who holds the right to use those 5,000 examples for training, and where did they come from?","Which engineers' calendar slots are free for the tuning workshops over the next month or so?","How many engineers will join the tuning work, and when will the plan be agreed with sponsors?","How will the tuned model be re-tested against the current setup and a baseline set before release?","Which public benchmark score will be cited in the next stakeholder update about the model?"], [0,3], "The data's source and rights, and a re-test against the current setup, are the decisions that change risk and value. NIST AI 600-1 asks for provenance checks and for verifying that fine-tuning does not weaken safety and security controls. Tools, staffing and a cited score do not show either.")
  );

  Object.assign(window.AIPO.why, {
    "fr051": ["Speed matters but says nothing about whether the pages are right, owned or current.","Settings change how well the lookup works, but not whether the source pages may be used or kept up to date.","Source, ownership and upkeep decide what the assistant can say, and a third of the pages have no owner.","A decline list sets limits but does not fix unowned or out-of-date source pages."],
    "fr052": ["It holds the data back until the permitted use is confirmed and still moves the work forward with a lighter method.","Training on data does not give a right to use it, and the question of permitted use remains.","The sample is still training on data whose permitted use is unconfirmed, and review after the fact is too late.","Removing names may not settle whether the use was allowed, and the data team does not decide that."],
    "fr053": ["A published score is not a result on your questions, and complaints arrive after users are affected.","Passed questions can fail on the new model, so a partial re-run misses new errors.","Ticket volume is an indirect signal and can hide wrong answers, and half the users meet the unproven model.","A changed model needs your own test set re-run against the agreed threshold before it replaces the old one."],
    "fr054": ["Answers on other topics can shift when the documents change, and review after release comes too late.","The documents are part of what the model is given, so the full set is re-run with new questions for the changed policy and the owner signs off.","Loading and finding the files shows the plumbing works, not that the answers are right.","Three answers are too few to show what changed, even with the owner's agreement."],
    "fr055": ["Paying directly from launch exposes real payments to a 1.5% error rate with review after the money is gone.","One region still pays directly, and daily monitoring finds wrong payments after they are made.","A person approves each refund while real results build, and wider autonomy is earned in steps.","A near-zero bar may never be met on a test set, and waiting delays learning from real use."],
    "fr056": ["It asks what the system is given, what shaped it and how your own tests can be re-run after changes.","Popularity and satisfaction scores do not show quality on your cases.","Certificates say little about answer quality or how changes are tested.","Feature speed does not address what the system is given or how quality is checked."],
    "fr057": ["A written instruction alone is not a reliable fix, and the order limit stays in place.","A lower limit still lets hidden text raise orders, and a weekly log check finds them late.","Switching off for everyone is an over-reaction when the harm can be limited by removing one capability.","It removes the action that makes the weakness costly, keeps the useful reading and adds cases that test for it."],
    "fr058": ["The source and rights to the examples decide whether training on them is allowed.","Calendar availability is a scheduling matter and says nothing about the data or the result.","Staffing and dates are planning facts, not questions about the data or the result.","A re-test against the current setup and a baseline shows whether tuning helped and whether controls held.","A cited public score does not show behaviour on your own cases."]
  });

  window.AIPO.cards.push(
    { id: "frc21", domain: "frame", front: "Name the four ways of building covered in this lesson and what each hands the model.", back: "Prompting (written instructions and examples), RAG (documents or records looked up at answer time), fine-tuning (further training on your examples) and agents (a system that can act or call other tools). These plain definitions are general practice, not from a source.\nTrap: judging a method by its name instead of asking what the model is given." },
    { id: "frc22", domain: "frame", front: "What should a PO ask when a team proposes adding retrieval?", back: "Which documents or records will be read, who owns and updates them, whether we may use them this way, and how lookup and answers will be tested.\nTrap: testing only the final answers and not the source data behind them." },
    { id: "frc23", domain: "frame", front: "Does fine-tuning settle whether you may use the data?", back: "No. Training uses the examples and does not create a right to them, so confirm permitted use with privacy or legal first. NIST AI 600-1 asks for checks on training and test data provenance (MS-2.5-005) and re-evaluation of fine-tuned models (MP-4.1-007). Confirming permitted use is general practice.\nTrap: treating the model as a place where the data stops being the data." },
    { id: "frc24", domain: "frame", front: "What must be re-tested after switching the underlying model or changing the retrieved documents?", back: "Your own test set, read by question type against the agreed threshold, plus new questions for what changed. NIST AI 600-1 MP-4.1-007 asks for re-evaluation of models fine-tuned or enhanced on third-party models; extending this to a model switch or a document change is practical judgement.\nTrap: re-testing only the questions that failed before." }
  );
})();
